        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T12:29:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But sysfs is NOT the current bluetooth user/kernel api, why is this ok to do it this way for JUST this one function?  Just add it through the normal user/kernel api instead of placing random sysfs files in userspace with no protection from users breaking things.",
      "parentUuid": "74334492_6f51cac8",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6e721fd1_8ce3c61e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T12:35:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is no so-called \"normal\" user/kernel api for Bluetooth. The MGMT api is used for BlueZ\u0027s userspace part to communicate with its kernel space part, and it\u0027s just not suitable with other usecase. We don\u0027t use BlueZ in Android.",
      "parentUuid": "8ebcd2a5_a6c0a2d5",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "435a40a3_d1ebad73",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T12:44:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But you use the same user/kernel api that bluez uses, otherwise how can you control bluetooth devices?  What\u0027s wrong with just adding another function there?\n\nAdding a single sysfs api feels very wrong, why not just follow how all othter bluetooth control interfaces work?",
      "parentUuid": "6e721fd1_8ce3c61e",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4accca71_1d37146e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T13:11:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We control the bluetooth device through the HCI_USER_CHANNEL raw socket, compose / serialize the packets in userspace and the kernel just forwards it to the chip.\n\nWe can\u0027t export this function through that socket because the protocol is defined in the Bluetooth core specification. Moreover, this sysfs reset function aims to recover the Bluetooth chip in a out-of-band way when the socket is not responding e.g. due to the chip firmware issue.",
      "parentUuid": "435a40a3_d1ebad73",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c3765603_84d4e3c1",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T14:22:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What about all of the custom \"non spec\" ioctls in `hci_sock_ioctl()`?  Why not add a \"reset device\" one there?",
      "parentUuid": "4accca71_1d37146e",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "60aa07f9_36bf823c",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T14:22:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "And doesn\u0027t `HCIDEVRESET` cover this already?",
      "parentUuid": "c3765603_84d4e3c1",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "323dc130_14a1f49b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T14:48:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e What about all of the custom \"non spec\" ioctls in `hci_sock_ioctl()`?\n\nSo they are not \"non spec\". They will be transferred into the standard HCI commands and sent to the chip. They are part of the BlueZ api used by BlueZ\u0027s debugging tools.\n\n\u003e And doesn\u0027t `HCIDEVRESET` cover this already?\n\nNo it doesn\u0027t. It only triggers the in-band recovery command defined in the Bluetooth core spec. We want an out-of-band one that can be helpful when the in-band one is not working.",
      "parentUuid": "60aa07f9_36bf823c",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2ae7aad1_e6e5955f",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-23T10:40:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the in-band reset does not work, how will the out-of-band one work instead?  The code paths are the same here in the end, so why do you now have to support two of them?  And once you go down this \"out of band\" api, where does it stop?  Making random new user/kernel apis does not seem like a good idea (also, how are you properly mapping the sysfs directory to the socket you are talking on?)",
      "parentUuid": "323dc130_14a1f49b",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c8619e5f_714adc59",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-23T16:42:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e If the in-band reset does not work, how will the out-of-band one work instead?\n\nBecause it is literally out-of-band. For example the packets could be send through UART, but this function could reset the chip through something that is entirely unrelated to UART like a special reset pin connected to GPIO.\n\n\u003e The code paths are the same here in the end, so why do you now have to support two of them?\n\nNo they are not the same. If they are the same then I won\u0027t need to send out this patch and the upstream owner won\u0027t accept this for sure.\n\n\u003e And once you go down this \"out of band\" api, where does it stop?\n\nThe out-of-band reset would usually cause the BT chip to entirely disappear from the bus, reconnect, and all driver initialization code are re-run.\n\n\u003e Making random new user/kernel apis does not seem like a good idea\n\nCould you please kindly tell what is a good api? IMO this is not so bad and there are some similar api in sysfs as well like the rfkill module, which simply allows the user to turn on/off the wireless antenna. And I just found I was wrong in the beginning - The attribute acl is set to root:root 0200 by default, so it can\u0027t be written by arbitrary user. Do you feel this api any better then?\n\n\u003e (also, how are you properly mapping the sysfs directory to the socket you are talking on?)\n\nAll connected BT chip has a index. When we create the socket we need to specify the index, and we can always find the device under /sys/class/bluetooth/hci\u003cindex\u003e.",
      "parentUuid": "2ae7aad1_e6e5955f",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
